out/
analysis/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# uv.lock IS tracked, deliberately. pyproject.toml gives ranges, and a
# range is a promise that every computer resolves the same way -- which
# is not true. `opencv-python-headless>=4.9` quietly resolved to 5.0,
# which removed CascadeClassifier, and face detection died without a
# word for weeks. The lock is what makes the next machine behave like
# this one, so it travels with the code.

# Your originals are read-only and often gigabytes. Git tracks the EDIT
# (film.yaml), not the footage -- that is what makes the history cheap
# enough to commit on every render. Back media/ up the way you back up
# photographs; do not ask git to do it.
media/
music/
film.yaml.bak
.lastfilm
caption_wrap_after.png
.Rproj.user
last_error.txt

# Which camera and microphone this particular machine has. Per-machine,
# not per-project, and never shared -- the next computer has different
# hardware with differently-spelled names.
.devices.json

# The thumbnail you upload beside the film. Same reasoning as media/:
# it is a picture, back it up the way you back up pictures.
cover/

# The shelf itself travels (see pack.py). Its contents are your music
# and your pictures -- same reasoning as media/ and cover/ above, and
# music/ and cover/ already match at any depth. The READ ME is written
# by `film library` on whichever machine needs it.
library/READ ME.txt
